On Sat, 10 Oct 2026 10:52:37 -0700, Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com> wrote:
> See output of a `just fetch` below. First I'd like to know why I haven't
> seen this before,

Two things had to be true together, and today was the first time
they were.

First, the recipe changed at 16:36 UTC today in "mail: just fetch
rebases only onto claude@submissions" (dd1db9aa2c31). Before that
commit, the rebase was `-b @ -d mail -d claude@submissions`. With
`mail` as a destination, everything under `mail` counted as already
in place, so your own commits never moved. Now only the ancestors of
claude@submissions count as in place. Every ancestor of @ outside
that set gets rebased, and that includes your published mail.

Second, my outbox has to be behind your published mail. Usually it
isn't, because each of my passes rebases the outbox onto
mail@upstream before pushing. But it was here. My last push before
your fetch was at 17:49 UTC. It was built on the list as of my 17:44
fetch, whose tip was "mail: just process-email". Your e0f91fe8 was
not on upstream yet then. So when you fetched at 17:52, e0f91fe8 was
an ancestor of @ but not of claude@submissions. The rebase tried to
move it, and jj refused because it was immutable.

That's a race, and my side can't close it. Whenever you push to
upstream between my fetch and my push, the next `just fetch` hits
it. If there is no such push, the recipe works.

> and then how to fix it.

The fix is posted as "[PATCH] mail: Keep just fetch from rewriting
published mail" [1]. It changes the rebase destination to

    heads((::@ & immutable()) | claude@submissions)

That is the newest published commit under @ or my outbox, whichever
is further along. When my outbox is built on your published tip, as
it usually is, this is just claude@submissions. Your mutable commits
move onto it and history stays linear, as you intended in
dd1db9aa2c31. In the race, the two heads have diverged. @ becomes a
merge of both, and nothing published is rewritten. The merge only
happens in that case.

I reproduced the failure in a scratch repo and tested the new
recipe there, in both the linear case and the race. The patch notes
give the details.

There are two other ways to handle the race. Neither seemed better
to me:

- Rebase my unmerged commits onto your tip instead. That keeps
  history linear, but in your repo claude@submissions is untracked
  and therefore immutable. It would need --ignore-immutable, and the
  old commits would stay visible under the remote bookmark until I
  push again.
- Keep failing in the race, but with a clear message, and run
  `just fetch` again after my next pass. That pass rebases my outbox
  onto your new tip. It stays linear but makes you wait on me.

If you'd rather have either of these than the merge, I'll revise
the patch.

